# FREXP(3)

## NAME

frexp, ldexp, modf - split into mantissa and exponent

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <math.h>

double frexp(double value, int *eptr);

double ldexp(double value, int exp);

double modf(double value, double *iptr);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Frexp* returns the mantissa of a double *value* as a double quantity, *x,* of magnitude less
than 1 and stores an integer *n* such that *value* = *x*\*2\*\**n* indirectly through *eptr.*

*Ldexp* returns the quantity *value*\*2\*\**exp.*

*Modf* returns the fractional part of *value* and stores the integer part indirectly through
*iptr.* **Note:** v7's page said "positive fractional part". Both parts carry the *sign of
value,* which is the split C11 sec 7.12.6.12 requires and the one trunc(3) and round(3) are
built on.

## SEE ALSO

exp(3), floor(3), ecvt(3), atof(3)

## BESM-6 NOTES

### These three are in libc, not libm

They are the exponent surgery the *conversions* need - atof(3), ecvt(3) and the printf(3)
engine - so a program gets them **without -lm,** which is also where v7 keeps them. <math.h>
declares them under a banner saying so.

### Both are exact, and libm leans on that

*Frexp* is twelve instructions and *ldexp* six, ports of the external c-compiler's Madlen
originals. There is no approximation in either: the exponent field is bits 48..42 of the word,
biased by 64, so *frexp* shifts it down and masks and *ldexp* ORs a new one in and multiplies.

**Ldexp does no range check whatever.** It keeps only the low seven bits of its argument, so an
out-of-range *exp* aliases modulo 128 and the result is nonsense rather than an error. That is
what exp(3)'s lower range gate stands between a caller and, and it is the reason that gate is
not cosmetic.

### modf knows where the fractional bits run out

Any magnitude at or above **2**40** is already integral in a 40-bit mantissa, so *modf* returns
a zero fraction there and the integer part unchanged. Below that the integer part fits in a
41-bit **int**, so a truncating round trip through **int** recovers it exactly.

That 2**40 is the constant sin(3) and pow(3) in libm key on by name, and it is defined here.

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/frexp.s, ldexp.s, modf.c
